WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Would you ever take a pay cut for a job that look worse on paper? Because we're taught to think that career progress means more money, a better title and a step up. But some of the most important moves I've made in my career have been sideways, and one of the moves took me entirely away from psychology for almost two years. So how do you know when a move that feels like it might be backwards or possibly sideways has potential to take you forwards. I'm Dr. Marianne Trent and this is Psychology Actually. Hi, welcome along to Psychology Actually. I am Dr. Marianne Trent, a qualified clinical psychologist. It's really lovely to have you here. I was inspired to do this episode after seeing a Reddit post

2
00:00:51.000 --> 00:01:32.000
and it really made me think about so many aspects of my own career, my own journey and that of supporting qualified psychologists, even advising people outside of psychology roles, when they're considering what they're going to do for work, what they should apply for, whether they should go for interviews. And I thought it might be useful to have a conversation about that here with you. So the post, in a nutshell, was that someone had found themselves in a Band 5 NHS assistant psychologist role. I've been there for almost 18 months. And now they're sort of at that point where they're like,

3
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:55.000
I think it's time to move on, but I'm not really seeing any other band five roles. I'm seeing maybe lots of band four roles start to come up. But I'm used to the money, and then I also used to get travel, so that kind of boosted it a little bit. And I'm not really sure what to do. So I guess I've got a couple of thoughts around that.

4
00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:48.000
And firstly, it's when you're looking to progress in your career, it might well be that you feel like really, in order to stand out on an application form, I'm probably going to need to get some extra skills, some extra experience, some enhanced level of depth of understanding on what I'm already doing that I think maybe, I might have reached the plateau for in my current role. And believe me, that does happen. And at that point, you might be like, could I do some other bits in this role? Could I speak to my manager? Could I think about doing, I don't know, some service or some audit or taking on a special project that helps me to gain more skills and experience?

5
00:02:48.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Maybe I might be able to start supervising a member of staff. Maybe I might be able to start putting together a group and then running that in conjunction with someone who might be qualified or another member of the team or even shadowing some different kind of different members of the MDT team if you're in that sort of service, that sort of role, so that you can really feel like you are still being shaped, you're still growing, you're still learning because I don't think is anything more stagnating than feeling like you're not. really getting anything new. Now of course energy-wise, sometimes that suits us. So when I was just back from having my second baby and when I was kind of going through some grief stuff with

6
00:03:36.000 --> 00:04:24.000
my dad, then energy-wise I probably wouldn't have been wanting to do things that were a very steep learning curve for me. So a little bit of variety was okay, but actually I didn't have the resources personally at that time to be doing more. You, might feel different. You might be like I've got all this energy, I've got all this enthusiasm, but I've either reached the plateau for this job, or when I'm asking for more roles, when I'm asking for more responsibility, when I'm asking for a little bit of kind of flavour and variety in the work that I'm doing, for whatever reason that isn't coming to me. You then get to decide, well, what am I going to do about that? Am I going to look elsewhere? And of course,

7
00:04:24.000 --> 00:05:12.000
none of us are in this to work for free. Even though we genuinely care about people, if we're working in mental health, I would hope that you're genuinely move by the distress of others. Because really, I think when we're working in mental health, that is really important because the money is not always amazing, right? So you have to have another why. But of course, if you've got fixed cost bills to pay, maybe your mortgage, your rent, you know, your travel costs, you might even have dependents that you need to help support or solely support, then actually dropping down from a band five role or a band seven role to a band six role to do training, that is and can be a very big consideration. Now with the current cost of living crisis,

8
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:59.000
then of course if you have been getting travel reimbursements, I don't think we're getting much more than actually the cost of the fuel at the moment. So if you're going to be in a base where you are not travelling, you're just going to your place of work, and then you're going home at the end of the day, then actually, if you're spending less on fuel, that kind of travel expense might not feature as much for you. It might just be like a psychological norm that you're used to seeing a certain amount of money come in, but of course some of that will have been money going out because you've had to pay for parking costs. You've had to pay for your fuel for your trips to get out and see your clients. Some of it might be a bit lifestyle

9
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:46.000
as well. Maybe if you are out and about, you're used to being able to work in an agile manner or work from home sometimes. And so some of that might be a little bit of a culture change to you as well. When I was freshly graduated from university, I actually took a completely unrelated role because I needed to prioritise my own needs above my career needs. So at that time, I was saving up to go travelling. So my primary concern was being able to be in a role where I could do as many hours as possible at a higher hourly rate than I'd already been on. So I was a home carer and I absolutely loved that work. But I couldn't suddenly invent more home care clients for the company. And so I kind of left the industry all together and then started.

10
00:06:46.000 --> 00:07:32.000
doing work as a temp worker in office buildings in Milton Keynes and the money for that was higher as an hourly rate and it also meant longer days so I was working nine I think until 6pm so long days and then there was also the potential to do overtime on a Saturday as well so that really did boost my income which then allowed me to save to be able to have the six months off to go around the world and that was you know of priority for me but it did mean I took almost two years out of what I was doing until I then found my way back into a relevant role because actually as soon as I got back from travelling, I went back to doing that temp work but someone had offered me a permanent job. So I went back to one of the teams that I'd been in,

11
00:07:32.000 --> 00:08:19.000
but I do not consider that wasted time. I consider that personal enrichment development, personal development for me and stuff that actually still really helps me on tricky days is reflecting on some of those incredible memories that I had when I was in my early 20s. I think something else that might be helpful to say is my experience of working as, for example, an assistant psychologist in a team is that often I felt like I was doing work that was higher up than kind of the band four I was doing. And a lot of the roles I was seeing advertised were maybe, you know, suitable for about than five people to be doing. but for whatever reason they were just banned four roles.

12
00:08:19.000 --> 00:09:02.000
So please don't think that it's like a demotion if you're applying for a band four role. And I know pay wise it might feel that way. But similarly, I've worked in band seven roles that really I was ready to be at an 8A, but there wasn't the budget to be able to kind of support a preceptorship shift. So it might be that you're in a role at the moment where you are gaining that seniority of experience, if you're looking at your key performance indicators, that you are gaining some of the skills that you would be able to say, well, I am at that level, but for whatever reason, there isn't the budget there to support that. So try not to be too led on the bandings, is what I would say.

13
00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:54.000
That said, of course, if you find yourself in a role where you're doing things vastly above your spine point, then do discuss that with your manager and your supervisor to consider whether rebranding might be an option or whether actually someone else in the team should be doing some of those roles. I think also it can be helpful to think about the kind of different reasons why we might want to optimise our career. And broadly speaking, I think they are. Money, learning, time, well-being, relationships and future options. So for me, it was not a case that I could always keep all six of those kind of ducks in a row, but it's allowing myself to prioritise one or two of those at any given time. And that might suit me for now, but it might not necessarily suit me forever. So it's

14
00:09:54.000 --> 00:10:39.000
really empowering myself to make the choice for whatever version of myself is present at this time. So like I said, if I'd been looking for new roles when I'd just lost my dad, then it might have been I'd prioritise a role that was higher paid so I could perhaps do fewer days a week and that was close to home so that it didn't impact on my relationships and didn't take me too long to get to. I would love your thoughts on this. How are you juggling your six components? Do let me know in the comments whether you're watching on YouTube or on Spotify. And people have been like, why don't you ever mention Apple Podcasts? I know lots of you listen on Apple Podcasts as well, but currently there isn't a facility to be able to drop episode-specific comments, but there is an opportunity

