What does my life need from my job right now? If nobody could see my job title or my salary, which role will I actually choose? And seeing as this is a pretty good reflective journal exercise, let's drop in one more for you to consider as well. Am I making this move because it genuinely is going to help me or because I'm frightened that my existing experience isn't enough? If it's the latter, you might find it helpful to talk to someone supportive, a supervisor, a manager, someone else who knows your position and understands the context in which you work. Because of course, we can't know that that is a genuine, genuine opinion that our brain is serving us up.

20
00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:53.000
It might just be a lot of fear, there might not necessarily be too much evidence for you to draw that conclusion. So I often think that getting a second opinion from someone that you genuinely trust and has insight into your situation is a really good idea. That said, if your career of choice is psychology and you're striving for the doctorate in clinical psychology, why not check out the aspiring psychologist membership, which you can join from just £30 a month with no minimum term. I do also offer therapy and coaching sessions too. So even if your career of choice is not psychology, I might still be able to help you to achieve your goals. So in summary, I think what I'd say is that our careers don't always move in kind of forward increments. It's not always a neat, linear upwards trajectory.

